      "text": "We start with the given equation:\n\n\\[16^{x^2 + y} + 16^{x + y^2} = 1.\\]\n\nFirst, note that \\(16\\) can be written as \\(2^4\\), so we can rewrite the equation as:\n\n\\[(2^4)^{x^2 + y} + (2^4)^{x + y^2} = 1,\\]\nwhich simplifies to:\n\n\\[2^{4(x^2 + y)} + 2^{4(x + y^2)} = 1.\\]\n\nFor the sum of two powers of 2 to be 1, one of the terms must be very small and the other must be very large. Since \\(2^k\\) is an integer for any real \\(k\\), the only way for the sum to be 1 is if one term is an extremely small positive number and the other term is an extremely large number. The only way this can happen is if both terms are equal to \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\) because:\n\n\\[2^{4(x^2 + y)} = \\frac{1}{2} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 2^{4(x + y^2)} = \\frac{1}{2}.\\]\n\nThis implies:\n\n\\[4(x^2 + y) = -1 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 4(x + y^2) = -1.\\]\n\nSimplifying these equations, we get:\n\n\\[x^2 + y = -\\frac{1}{4} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad x + y^2 = -\\frac{1}{4}.\\]\n\nWe now have a system of two equations:\n\n1. \\(x^2 + y = -\\frac{1}{4}\\),\n2. \\(x + y^2 = -\\frac{1}{4}\\).\n\nWe can subtract the first equation from the second to eliminate \\(y\\):\n\n\\[(x + y^2) - (x^2 + y) = -\\frac{1}{4} - \\left(-\\frac{1}{4}\\right),\\]\nwhich simplifies to:\n\n\\[y^2 - x^2 = 0,\\]\nor\n\n\\[(y - x)(y + x) = 0.\\]\n\nThis gives us two cases: \\(y = x\\) or \\(y = -x\\).\n\n**Case 1: \\(y = x\\)**\n\nSubstitute \\(y = x\\) into the first equation:\n\n\\[x^2 + x = -\\frac{1}{4},\\]\nwhich simplifies to:\n\n\\[x^2 + x + \\frac{1}{4} = 0.\\]\n\nThis can be rewritten as:\n\n\\[(x + \\frac{1}{2})^2 = 0,\\]\nso\n\n\\[x = -\\frac{1}{2}.\\]\n\nThen \\(y = -\\frac{1}{2}\\). So one solution is \\((x, y) = \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}, -\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\).\n\n**Case 2: \\(y = -x\\)**\n\nSubstitute \\(y = -x\\) into the first equation:\n\n\\[x^2 - x = -\\frac{1}{4},\\]\nwhich simplifies to:\n\n\\[x^2 - x + \\frac{1}{4} = 0.\\]\n\nThis can be rewritten as:\n\n\\[(x - \\frac{1}{2})^2 = 0,\\]\nso\n\n\\[x = \\frac{1}{2}.\\]\n\nThen \\(y = -\\frac{1}{2}\\). So another solution is \\((x, y) = \\left(\\frac{1}{2}, -\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\).\n\nTherefore, the only solutions are \\(\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}, -\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\) and \\(\\left(\\frac{1}{2}, -\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\). There are 2 ordered pairs that satisfy the equation.\n\nThe final answer is:\n\n\\[\\boxed{2}.\\]",
